d catcheth away that which was sown in his heart. This is he which received seed by the way side.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙvBut he that received the seed into stony places, the same is he that heareth the word, and anon with joy receiveth it;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ“yet hath he not root in himself, but dureth for a while: for when tribulation or persecution ariseth because of the word, by and by he is offended.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ±He also that received seed among the thorns is he that heareth the word; and the care of this world, and the deceitfulness of riches, choke the word, and he becometh unfruitful.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ½But he that received seed into the good ground is he that heareth the word, and understandeth it; which also beareth fruit, and bringeth forth, some an hundredfold, some sixty, some thirty.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses—„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnother parable put he forth unto them, saying, The kingdom of heaven is likened unto a man which sowed good seed in his field:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙVbut while men slept, his enemy came and sowed tares among the wheat, and went his way.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙXBut when the blade was sprung up, and brought forth fruit, then appeared the tares also.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙŠSo the servants of the householder came and said unto him, Sir, didst not thou sow good seed in thy field? from whence then hath it tares?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙuHe said unto them, An enemy hath done this. The servants said unto him, Wilt thou then that we go and gather them up?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙYBut he said, Nay; lest while ye gather up the tares, ye root up also the wheat with them.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÍLet both grow together until the harvest: and in the time of harvest I will say to the reapers, Gather ye together first the tares, and bind them in bundles to burn them: but gather the wheat into my barn.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse!¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ“Another parable put he forth unto them, saying, The kingdom of heaven is like to a grain of mustard seed, which a man took, and sowed in his field:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ·which indeed is the least of all seeds: but when it is grown, it is the greatest among herbs, and becometh a tree, so that the birds of the air come and lodge in the branches thereof.¦layout ªlast_verse „¥verse!¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¢Another parable spake he unto them; The kingdom of heaven is like unto leaven, which a woman took, and hid in three measures of meal, till the whole was leavened.¦layout ªlast_verse!…¤book'§chapter«first_verse"ªlast_verse#¦verses’„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙjAll these things spake Jesus unto the multitude in parables; and without a parable spake he not unto them:¦layout ªlast_verse"„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines“‚¤textÙCThat it might be fulfilled which was spoken by the prophet, saying,¦layout ‚¤textÙ!I will open my mouth in parables;¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙQI will utter things which have been kept secret from the foundation of the world.¦layout§quote_1ªlast_verse#…¤book'§chapter«first_verse$ªlast_verse+¦verses˜„¥verse$¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙœThen Jesus sent the multitude away, and went into the house: and his disciples came unto him, saying, Declare unto us the parable of the tares of the field.¦layout ªlast_verse$„¥verse%¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙOHe answered and said unto them, He that soweth the good seed is the Son of man;¦layout ªlast_verse%„¥verse&¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙxthe field is the world; th